[{:db/id 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091946 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-68e7c276-c403-5f7f-bb19-14fc56277444" "question-c8719458-2bd9-576f-821e-1af82c528d6c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a7a2bab2-b83f-574b-87f1-a6024a7e2d15" "question-55ca9374-3367-5974-bb86-994acf801013"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44852747-843f-5a59-aaa4-c3f273f2d780" "question-2357828d-1c68-5412-b5ab-9d7c9a1f8663"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091948 :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\nII. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272247 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n\nII. $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3" :question/id #uuid "17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141235" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3642f741-bfcb-537b-8055-8377c2f4a23c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3642f741-bfcb-537b-8055-8377c2f4a23c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3642f741-bfcb-537b-8055-8377c2f4a23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" "answer-972d63cb-deb1-504d-983c-3afd89efb163" "answer-e1cafff7-1db2-590c-918b-31d40bb1ec62" "answer-5f573ec0-e420-5d5a-bf15-12b9f19228c1" "answer-db22347e-ef69-54bb-bdf3-e3c186808327"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" :answer/id #uuid "22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-972d63cb-deb1-504d-983c-3afd89efb163" :answer/id #uuid "972d63cb-deb1-504d-983c-3afd89efb163" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1cafff7-1db2-590c-918b-31d40bb1ec62" :answer/id #uuid "e1cafff7-1db2-590c-918b-31d40bb1ec62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f573ec0-e420-5d5a-bf15-12b9f19228c1" :answer/id #uuid "5f573ec0-e420-5d5a-bf15-12b9f19228c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db22347e-ef69-54bb-bdf3-e3c186808327" :answer/id #uuid "db22347e-ef69-54bb-bdf3-e3c186808327" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68e7c276-c403-5f7f-bb19-14fc56277444" :question/id #uuid "68e7c276-c403-5f7f-bb19-14fc56277444" :question/math-academy-id "q-277897" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{7}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{7+8}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" :answer/id #uuid "f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8719458-2bd9-576f-821e-1af82c528d6c" :question/id #uuid "c8719458-2bd9-576f-821e-1af82c528d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141256" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{3+9}={x}^{12}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $12.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-18301aa5-8122-537f-afa8-0033da27bf6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-18301aa5-8122-537f-afa8-0033da27bf6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "18301aa5-8122-537f-afa8-0033da27bf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" "answer-2219b244-05db-51b1-afdc-21618a8ff9db" "answer-24ca3551-0275-501a-8224-d0c29cf4662c" "answer-dfa2f6a3-912d-5f28-9899-584bb9cb2f53" "answer-13fc7668-35a7-52b2-8d6b-1ef66cb2dee3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" :answer/id #uuid "0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2219b244-05db-51b1-afdc-21618a8ff9db" :answer/id #uuid "2219b244-05db-51b1-afdc-21618a8ff9db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ca3551-0275-501a-8224-d0c29cf4662c" :answer/id #uuid "24ca3551-0275-501a-8224-d0c29cf4662c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfa2f6a3-912d-5f28-9899-584bb9cb2f53" :answer/id #uuid "dfa2f6a3-912d-5f28-9899-584bb9cb2f53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13fc7668-35a7-52b2-8d6b-1ef66cb2dee3" :answer/id #uuid "13fc7668-35a7-52b2-8d6b-1ef66cb2dee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7a2bab2-b83f-574b-87f1-a6024a7e2d15" :question/id #uuid "a7a2bab2-b83f-574b-87f1-a6024a7e2d15" :question/math-academy-id "q-269923" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $12$,  the highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $12.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{12}$ and $Q={x}^{12}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{12}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad249e65-93ce-5e24-9e7f-3d95d55441e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad249e65-93ce-5e24-9e7f-3d95d55441e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad249e65-93ce-5e24-9e7f-3d95d55441e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" :answer/id #uuid "b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-55ca9374-3367-5974-bb86-994acf801013" :question/id #uuid "55ca9374-3367-5974-bb86-994acf801013" :question/math-academy-id "q-277912" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=9{x}^{7}$ and $Q=-9{x}^{7}-4.$ Then $P+Q=-4,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae" :answer-field/id #uuid "37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" :answer/id #uuid "8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44852747-843f-5a59-aaa4-c3f273f2d780" :question/id #uuid "44852747-843f-5a59-aaa4-c3f273f2d780" :question/math-academy-id "q-111389" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $2$\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+x$ and $Q=-{x}^{3}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{3}+x-{x}^{3}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2},$ which is a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=1+{x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{1+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1,$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3b7845b-8ad3-57c0-a6ae-28437acfe3a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3b7845b-8ad3-57c0-a6ae-28437acfe3a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b7845b-8ad3-57c0-a6ae-28437acfe3a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" "answer-2a8cb1de-ccb8-5910-9ec5-4dba43048b8b" "answer-045d0a65-b7d8-5bfb-9b43-906b5283f84a" "answer-79f71a19-7850-565d-91b5-d77d8197d35f" "answer-14893a56-3116-5bfd-b0d1-990957ad1998"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" :answer/id #uuid "b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a8cb1de-ccb8-5910-9ec5-4dba43048b8b" :answer/id #uuid "2a8cb1de-ccb8-5910-9ec5-4dba43048b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-045d0a65-b7d8-5bfb-9b43-906b5283f84a" :answer/id #uuid "045d0a65-b7d8-5bfb-9b43-906b5283f84a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79f71a19-7850-565d-91b5-d77d8197d35f" :answer/id #uuid "79f71a19-7850-565d-91b5-d77d8197d35f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14893a56-3116-5bfd-b0d1-990957ad1998" :answer/id #uuid "14893a56-3116-5bfd-b0d1-990957ad1998" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2357828d-1c68-5412-b5ab-9d7c9a1f8663" :question/id #uuid "2357828d-1c68-5412-b5ab-9d7c9a1f8663" :question/math-academy-id "q-111386" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $8,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $32$\n\nIII. $\\frac{Q}{P}$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{8}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q,$ still contains ${x}^{8}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $8.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{4+8}={x}^{12},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $12.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}$ and $Q={x}^{8}+1.$ Then, $\\frac{Q}{P}=\\frac{{x}^{8}+1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d08c9c2d-c678-54bf-8b79-6e361af4291c" "answer-5582da46-e860-5de6-9a80-bf70a2d5dbc2" "answer-8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" "answer-5eba3768-372e-57d2-9f12-5041b396542f" "answer-f7c7fe9a-33a1-5066-a984-2b33bf4beec0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d08c9c2d-c678-54bf-8b79-6e361af4291c" :answer/id #uuid "d08c9c2d-c678-54bf-8b79-6e361af4291c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5582da46-e860-5de6-9a80-bf70a2d5dbc2" :answer/id #uuid "5582da46-e860-5de6-9a80-bf70a2d5dbc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" :answer/id #uuid "8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eba3768-372e-57d2-9f12-5041b396542f" :answer/id #uuid "5eba3768-372e-57d2-9f12-5041b396542f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7c7fe9a-33a1-5066-a984-2b33bf4beec0" :answer/id #uuid "f7c7fe9a-33a1-5066-a984-2b33bf4beec0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093471 :tutorial/content "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n*The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092437 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
